<Project>

  <!--
    Stage OpenConsole.exe for a PROJECT REFERENCE consumer.

    Porta.Pty.targets does this for package consumers, and cannot do it for these: it runs in the
    consumer's build, and a project reference imports none of our build/ files. So the staging has to
    happen in OUR build, and land in a layout the consumer inherits.

    What the consumer inherits is the point. ConptyRequires*Host puts the hosts at our output ROOT,
    which reaches the consumer's root — right for a RID-specific consumer, where conpty.dll is
    flattened to the root beside them. A PORTABLE consumer keeps the runtimes/ tree, so its conpty.dll
    lands in runtimes/win-x64/native/ with the hosts still at the root: apart, which is the one
    arrangement that does not work. conpty.dll looks for its host BESIDE ITSELF, finds nothing, and
    falls back to in-box conhost without failing or warning.

    That fallback is invisible until the consumer is a terminal emulator. conhost DRAWS the child's
    output instead of relaying it, so every sequence it does not model is consumed on the way past.
    Measured with a child emitting one query per family through a portable project-reference consumer:
    CSI arrived; XTGETTCAP, DECRQSS, OSC 10/11, OSC 52 and the Kitty graphics APC did not. The child
    concludes the terminal cannot do any of it.

    So both layouts are staged, and the consumer uses whichever matches the build it did. The cost is
    the hosts twice in a project-reference output, which is a development build by definition.

    None with Link, NOT ReferenceCopyLocalPaths, and the difference is the whole reason this file
    exists. A project reference propagates through GetCopyToOutputDirectoryItems, which reads items
    assigned a target path at EVALUATION time; ReferenceCopyLocalPaths added by a target reaches our
    own output and stops there. Measured: staged that way, our bin had
    runtimes/win-x64/native/x64/OpenConsole.exe and the consumer's did not.
  -->

  <!--
    PkgMicrosoft_Windows_Console_ConPTY comes from GeneratePathProperty on the PackageReference, and
    names the package directory that RESTORE actually chose. Composing the path instead — package root
    plus id plus version — needs the version as a literal, which is a second place to update when the
    reference moves and no error when it is missed: Exists() goes false, no item is created, and the
    build succeeds having staged nothing.

    Skipped when THIS project is built RID-specific, where native assets flatten to the root and
    ConptyRequires*Host has already put the hosts there. That is a narrower saving than it looks: a
    consumer's RuntimeIdentifier does not reach this build — measured, a clean win-x64 consumer builds
    Porta.Pty portable and receives both copies. So a RID consumer carries 2.1MB it will not load, and
    the cure for that is a build-time question this file cannot see the answer to.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(PortaPtyStageProjectReferenceHost)' != 'false' And '$(RuntimeIdentifier)' == ''">
    <None Include="$(PkgMicrosoft_Windows_Console_ConPTY)/build/native/runtimes/x64/OpenConsole.exe"
          Condition="Exists('$(PkgMicrosoft_Windows_Console_ConPTY)/build/native/runtimes/x64/OpenConsole.exe')">
      <Link>runtimes\win-x64\native\x64\OpenConsole.exe</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
      <CopyToPublishDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToPublishDirectory>
      <Pack>false</Pack>
    </None>
    <None Include="$(PkgMicrosoft_Windows_Console_ConPTY)/build/native/runtimes/arm64/OpenConsole.exe"
          Condition="Exists('$(PkgMicrosoft_Windows_Console_ConPTY)/build/native/runtimes/arm64/OpenConsole.exe')">
      <Link>runtimes\win-arm64\native\arm64\OpenConsole.exe</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
      <CopyToPublishDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToPublishDirectory>
      <Pack>false</Pack>
    </None>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
